1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,630
An innkeeper with twenty rooms
is closing out a reservation.

2
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:08,040
Good guest. Three nights, tipped
housekeeping, asked about the trail behind

3
00:00:08,080 --> 00:00:10,690
the property. The kind of
person she'd like to see again.

4
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:13,110
So she goes to send a thank you note.

5
00:00:13,270 --> 00:00:16,650
And the email on the
reservation isn't his.

6
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,370
It's a string of letters and
numbers ending in guest.booking.com.

7
00:00:21,740 --> 00:00:22,740
A relay.

8
00:00:23,340 --> 00:00:26,960
A week or so after checkout,
the relay stops working.

9
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:32,010
A year later the same guest books the
same room, through the same website.

10
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:36,490
And she pays the site the same
commission she paid the first time.

11
00:00:36,760 --> 00:00:39,420
For a guest she already knows by name.

12
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:45,210
There's a service a small inn would pay
for hiding inside that second commission.

13
00:00:45,720 --> 00:00:49,640
And there's one piece of arithmetic that
decides whether it's worth your time to

14
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:50,680
sell it.

15
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:54,220
I couldn't find anyone selling
it who'd done that arithmetic.

16
00:00:54,880 --> 00:00:57,610
So what did that second
stay actually cost her,

17
00:00:58,000 --> 00:00:59,670
and who gets paid to stop it?

18
00:01:00,130 --> 00:01:04,950
This is The Operator Economy, where we
show you how to use AI to build, own, and

19
00:01:04,990 --> 00:01:07,710
operate a sustainable business of one.

20
00:01:07,750 --> 00:01:10,390
Today we're building a
direct-booking practice.

21
00:01:10,450 --> 00:01:14,510
It helps a small hotel get its returning
guests to book direct, instead of paying a

22
00:01:14,550 --> 00:01:16,790
booking site to meet them again.

23
00:01:16,830 --> 00:01:20,430
By the end you'll be able to take four
numbers from a small property and know

24
00:01:20,470 --> 00:01:24,570
what it can honestly pay for the job,
and whether that's worth your time.

25
00:01:24,610 --> 00:01:28,910
Start with how a small inn fills its
rooms, because it's worth being fair to

26
00:01:28,950 --> 00:01:30,660
the system before we pick at it.

27
00:01:30,970 --> 00:01:36,120
A traveller opens Booking.com or Expedia,
types a town and some dates, and compares.

28
00:01:36,350 --> 00:01:38,140
So the inn lists there.

29
00:01:38,180 --> 00:01:41,470
The site puts the property in front of
people the inn could never reach on its

30
00:01:41,510 --> 00:01:44,630
own and takes a commission on every stay.

31
00:01:44,670 --> 00:01:48,360
She rents an audience she couldn't
build, and that's a fair trade.

32
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:50,680
The commission is where it gets layered.

33
00:01:51,190 --> 00:01:55,770
Booking.com's own partner help says it's a
set percentage of the whole booking, fees

34
00:01:55,810 --> 00:02:00,350
included, charged at checkout, and that it
goes up if the property joins one of its

35
00:02:00,390 --> 00:02:01,790
visibility programmes.

36
00:02:01,890 --> 00:02:03,990
It doesn't publish the base rate.

37
00:02:04,030 --> 00:02:07,930
Trade reporting puts that around fifteen
percent in most European markets.

38
00:02:08,120 --> 00:02:11,650
The preferred badge and the visibility
booster add to it, and the Genius

39
00:02:11,690 --> 00:02:15,280
programme is a discount the
hotel funds from its own rate.

40
00:02:15,320 --> 00:02:17,310
Then three things changed.

41
00:02:17,850 --> 00:02:22,950
Cloudbeds, which sells hotel software,
reports the share of independent hotel

42
00:02:22,990 --> 00:02:27,870
reservations coming through these sites
rose in twenty twenty five, to about sixty

43
00:02:27,910 --> 00:02:29,910
three percent worldwide.

44
00:02:29,950 --> 00:02:35,430
Early in twenty twenty six, Booking.com
changed how Genius placement works, so the

45
00:02:35,470 --> 00:02:38,800
minimum discount no longer
buys the visibility it used to.

46
00:02:39,370 --> 00:02:44,350
And in Europe, more than fifteen thousand
hotels have joined a damages claim against

47
00:02:44,390 --> 00:02:47,200
Booking.com over price parity clauses.

48
00:02:47,530 --> 00:02:51,120
That's Europe, not here,
but hotels are acting at scale.

49
00:02:51,710 --> 00:02:52,760
Who feels it?

50
00:02:53,110 --> 00:02:57,370
The owner running twenty to forty rooms
with no marketing hire and a front desk

51
00:02:57,410 --> 00:02:58,690
that's short staffed.

52
00:02:59,090 --> 00:03:02,550
About two thirds of the hotels the
American Hotel and Lodging Association

53
00:03:02,590 --> 00:03:05,260
surveyed reported staffing shortages.

54
00:03:05,799 --> 00:03:07,650
Which brings us to the question.

55
00:03:07,980 --> 00:03:11,549
The site introduces the guest
and keeps the guest's email.

56
00:03:11,750 --> 00:03:16,110
When that guest comes back, who at the inn
is paid to make sure the second booking

57
00:03:16,150 --> 00:03:17,150
comes to the inn?

58
00:03:17,710 --> 00:03:22,150
And if the answer is nobody, can
somebody from outside get paid to do it?

59
00:03:22,190 --> 00:03:23,600
Nobody at the inn is.

60
00:03:23,990 --> 00:03:25,400
And that's not a lapse.

61
00:03:25,840 --> 00:03:29,690
The site is paid per introduction,
so it has no reason to hand over the

62
00:03:29,730 --> 00:03:30,760
relationship.

63
00:03:31,130 --> 00:03:35,570
The desk is short staffed, so writing to
last year's guests is the job that never

64
00:03:35,610 --> 00:03:36,610
gets done.

65
00:03:36,930 --> 00:03:41,470
Which leaves the property paying an
introduction fee to meet its own regulars.

66
00:03:41,510 --> 00:03:45,260
That gap is a job. I think
it can be sold from outside.

67
00:03:45,680 --> 00:03:49,790
But only for what it recovers,
and what it recovers is a number.

68
00:03:50,390 --> 00:03:53,270
By the end you'll be able
to compute it for any inn.

69
00:03:53,310 --> 00:03:57,430
First, how much of the inn's
business is actually rented.

70
00:03:57,470 --> 00:04:01,410
Cloudbeds, from about ninety million
bookings, puts the share of independent

71
00:04:01,450 --> 00:04:06,720
hotel bookings arriving through the sites
at about sixty three percent, worldwide.

72
00:04:07,330 --> 00:04:12,650
Cloudbeds sells the software hotels use to
take bookings direct, so it has a view on

73
00:04:12,690 --> 00:04:15,200
this the way a dentist
has a view on flossing.

74
00:04:15,890 --> 00:04:21,830
For balance, HOTREC, the European hotel
association, studied three thousand hotels

75
00:04:21,870 --> 00:04:26,520
and found direct channels still carried
about half of overnight stays there.

76
00:04:27,090 --> 00:04:32,630
So somewhere between half and two
thirds of a small property's bookings

77
00:04:32,670 --> 00:04:34,740
arrive with a commission attached.

78
00:04:35,130 --> 00:04:39,810
And nobody has published what a thirty
room inn in the United States actually

79
00:04:39,850 --> 00:04:43,460
runs. That's the first
thing the audit measures.

80
00:04:43,500 --> 00:04:45,610
Same data, one more thing.

81
00:04:45,650 --> 00:04:49,580
Bookings through the sites cancelled
at about twenty two percent.

82
00:04:50,090 --> 00:04:52,410
Direct bookings, about eleven.

83
00:04:52,450 --> 00:04:56,340
So the rented booking costs
more and shows up less often.

84
00:04:56,790 --> 00:05:02,270
The inn is renting most of its demand, at
a price, and doesn't know its own number.

85
00:05:02,850 --> 00:05:04,710
Second, what the site keeps.

86
00:05:05,050 --> 00:05:07,340
Old inns had a guest book by the door.

87
00:05:07,590 --> 00:05:12,030
You signed it, and the innkeeper had your
name, where you'd come from, and a reason

88
00:05:12,070 --> 00:05:15,610
to write to you. That book was the inn's.

89
00:05:15,650 --> 00:05:21,110
Booking.com's own partner help says it
doesn't share private email addresses.

90
00:05:21,150 --> 00:05:26,670
Both sides see an alias ending in
guest.booking.com, and partners are asked

91
00:05:26,710 --> 00:05:29,220
to keep the conversation on the platform.

92
00:05:29,460 --> 00:05:33,550
The trade press puts the messaging window
at about seven days after checkout, and

93
00:05:33,590 --> 00:05:35,880
Expedia's at about forty five.

94
00:05:36,270 --> 00:05:39,420
So the guest book now
lives at the booking site.

95
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:43,220
The inn served the guest
and doesn't have his email.

96
00:05:43,750 --> 00:05:48,620
Which means the recovery job has a
design constraint before it has a tool.

97
00:05:48,910 --> 00:05:53,230
You can't win a guest back from a relay
address, and under the site's rules you're

98
00:05:53,270 --> 00:05:58,240
not supposed to try. What the inn
needs first isn't an email tool.

99
00:05:58,390 --> 00:06:03,770
It's a guest book of its own, at the
front desk, that the guest chooses to

100
00:06:03,810 --> 00:06:06,230
sign. Fair question here.

101
00:06:06,270 --> 00:06:10,070
If the parts to fix this are cheap,
why doesn't the inn just do it?

102
00:06:10,470 --> 00:06:15,710
The parts are cheap. Google will show a
free official site link beside the site

103
00:06:15,750 --> 00:06:19,820
rates for a property connected through
a partner, and charges nothing for it.

104
00:06:20,190 --> 00:06:24,170
A small property booking engine runs from
around thirty dollars a month to about a

105
00:06:24,210 --> 00:06:25,280
hundred and nine.

106
00:06:25,870 --> 00:06:28,000
The obstacle is the front desk.

107
00:06:28,190 --> 00:06:33,260
At a twenty room inn, the person who'd set
up the free link, check the direct rate,

108
00:06:33,390 --> 00:06:37,270
write the thank you, and read the
reservation report is the same person

109
00:06:37,310 --> 00:06:39,960
checking in the guest
and finding a plumber.

110
00:06:40,430 --> 00:06:43,020
So the direct path isn't a system.

111
00:06:43,210 --> 00:06:46,720
It's a list of small jobs,
and the list belongs to nobody.

112
00:06:47,170 --> 00:06:49,090
Which raises the obvious objection.

113
00:06:49,230 --> 00:06:52,820
If this were worth paying for,
somebody would already be selling it.

114
00:06:53,290 --> 00:06:55,320
Somebody is, at two scales.

115
00:06:55,890 --> 00:07:00,940
Independent hotels big enough to justify
it hire outsourced revenue managers,

116
00:07:01,090 --> 00:07:05,500
outside specialists who run pricing and
channel strategy on a monthly retainer.

117
00:07:06,090 --> 00:07:08,090
Almost all of them quote privately.

118
00:07:09,160 --> 00:07:14,350
And there are hotel marketing agencies
aimed squarely at independents, inns, and

119
00:07:14,390 --> 00:07:15,390
small resorts.

120
00:07:15,910 --> 00:07:19,000
One publishes its entry price,
fifteen hundred dollars a month,

121
00:07:19,510 --> 00:07:24,660
and lists past guest email and direct
share reporting among the things it does.

122
00:07:25,610 --> 00:07:28,110
And above it all sit the sites themselves,

123
00:07:28,470 --> 00:07:33,850
which by their own filings each ran more
than a hundred billion dollars of bookings

124
00:07:33,890 --> 00:07:37,220
in twenty twenty five, blended
across everything they sell.

125
00:07:37,780 --> 00:07:40,290
That's the floor under this conversation.

126
00:07:40,930 --> 00:07:46,350
So the job is real and it's paid for,
by properties big enough to fund it.

127
00:07:46,390 --> 00:07:49,780
I could not find anyone selling
it to the twenty room inn.

128
00:07:50,590 --> 00:07:54,970
It's that the inn can only pay what the
job recovers, and nobody selling this has

129
00:07:55,010 --> 00:07:57,020
published what that is.

130
00:07:57,060 --> 00:07:59,640
So let's compute it. Here's the model.

131
00:08:00,030 --> 00:08:05,330
It's illustrative, I'm making the inn up,
and every input is one you'd swap for the

132
00:08:05,370 --> 00:08:06,810
real property's numbers.

133
00:08:07,430 --> 00:08:10,800
Twenty rooms. A hundred and
eighty dollars a night on average.

134
00:08:11,110 --> 00:08:13,320
Seventy percent full across the year.

135
00:08:13,690 --> 00:08:16,980
That's about nine hundred and twenty
thousand dollars of room revenue.

136
00:08:17,350 --> 00:08:22,210
Say sixty three percent of that comes
through the sites, the Cloudbeds figure.

137
00:08:22,250 --> 00:08:25,920
That's about five hundred and eighty
thousand dollars of rented bookings.

138
00:08:26,290 --> 00:08:30,850
At an all in commission of eighteen to
twenty two percent, my assumption from the

139
00:08:30,890 --> 00:08:35,290
reported bands, the inn is paying the
sites roughly a hundred and five to a

140
00:08:35,330 --> 00:08:37,289
hundred and thirty
thousand dollars a year.

141
00:08:37,770 --> 00:08:40,520
That's where the old version
of this argument stops.

142
00:08:40,630 --> 00:08:44,270
It's the wrong number, because the
inn is never going to move all of it.

143
00:08:44,310 --> 00:08:48,960
It needs the sites. The real question
is what a recovery service can shift.

144
00:08:49,330 --> 00:08:54,450
Say it moves ten points of the mix, from
sixty three percent rented to fifty three.

145
00:08:55,240 --> 00:08:58,690
That's about ninety two thousand
dollars of revenue now booked direct.

146
00:08:59,010 --> 00:09:03,230
Commission saved on that, at eighteen to
twenty two percent, is about sixteen and a

147
00:09:03,270 --> 00:09:05,590
half to twenty thousand dollars a year.

148
00:09:05,630 --> 00:09:09,830
Take off the cost of the direct channel
itself, call it three and a half to five

149
00:09:09,870 --> 00:09:12,660
percent, the low end being
one vendor's own figure.

150
00:09:12,950 --> 00:09:16,250
What's left is roughly twelve to
seventeen thousand dollars a year.

151
00:09:16,500 --> 00:09:19,060
A thousand to fourteen
hundred dollars a month.

152
00:09:19,100 --> 00:09:23,530
That's the ceiling. It's the most a twenty
room inn can rationally pay anyone to do

153
00:09:23,570 --> 00:09:26,180
this job, and the retainer
has to sit under it.

154
00:09:26,610 --> 00:09:27,670
Two things move it.

155
00:09:28,070 --> 00:09:31,670
A forty room property doubles it,
to roughly two thousand to twenty eight

156
00:09:31,710 --> 00:09:36,330
hundred a month. A five point shift
instead of ten halves it, to about five to

157
00:09:36,370 --> 00:09:37,370
seven hundred.

158
00:09:37,610 --> 00:09:40,690
The ten points is a hypothesis
nobody has measured.

159
00:09:40,730 --> 00:09:44,730
But the ceiling tells you, in four
numbers, whether a property can afford you

160
00:09:44,770 --> 00:09:46,540
before you've spent an hour on it.

161
00:09:47,010 --> 00:09:51,960
Now picture the company that does this
job, before we shrink it to a first sale.

162
00:09:52,370 --> 00:09:54,090
A direct-booking practice.

163
00:09:54,130 --> 00:09:58,990
One person working for a handful of small
properties at once, owning the three jobs

164
00:09:59,030 --> 00:10:01,290
the desk doesn't have time for.

165
00:10:01,330 --> 00:10:03,590
Findable, so the free link is live.

166
00:10:03,630 --> 00:10:06,580
Bookable, so the direct
rate matches the site.

167
00:10:07,070 --> 00:10:11,590
Remembered, which is the guest book,
the thank you, the reason to come back,

168
00:10:11,950 --> 00:10:15,210
with the guest's permission
and inside the sites' rules.

169
00:10:15,590 --> 00:10:17,460
And one number to the owner every month.

170
00:10:17,670 --> 00:10:20,630
Direct share of bookings,
before and after.

171
00:10:21,190 --> 00:10:26,250
At maturity that's six or so properties,
each priced under its own ceiling, and a

172
00:10:26,290 --> 00:10:30,610
practice that learns from its own audits
where in the room count the job starts to

173
00:10:30,650 --> 00:10:31,650
pay.

174
00:10:31,810 --> 00:10:34,620
Nobody has built that and
published what it earns.

175
00:10:35,040 --> 00:10:38,640
That's what makes it an
opportunity and not a category.

176
00:10:39,690 --> 00:10:41,900
So that's the business.

177
00:10:42,270 --> 00:10:45,680
The question now is whether
you're the person to build it.

178
00:10:46,170 --> 00:10:50,170
What this job actually needs isn't
a revenue management credential.

179
00:10:50,210 --> 00:10:53,270
It needs somebody who can write a thank
you an innkeeper would be proud to have

180
00:10:53,310 --> 00:10:57,470
sent, because a recovery message that
reads like a newsletter with a name tag on

181
00:10:57,510 --> 00:10:59,800
it loses the guest faster than silence.

182
00:11:00,370 --> 00:11:03,293
And somebody who can sit across
from an owner and talk about money.

183
00:11:03,333 --> 00:11:07,893
I spent ten years in hospitality,
including Ace Hotel and Standard Hotels.

184
00:11:07,933 --> 00:11:10,813
I know this business from the inside out.

185
00:11:10,853 --> 00:11:13,803
I haven't sold this
particular service, though.

186
00:11:14,023 --> 00:11:17,373
What a thirty room inn will pay for it
is still something I'd have to test.

187
00:11:18,167 --> 00:11:22,007
Start with properties you can drive
to and owners you can sit across from.

188
00:11:22,047 --> 00:11:25,177
If you've worked in hospitality,
or you know an innkeeper, start there.

189
00:11:25,827 --> 00:11:29,437
The advantage is being let into
the back office and the guest book.

190
00:11:29,687 --> 00:11:31,856
That's a relationship, not a tool.

191
00:11:32,467 --> 00:11:35,857
What does the machine do,
then, and what do you do?

192
00:11:36,427 --> 00:11:38,737
The software does the repetitive half.

193
00:11:38,967 --> 00:11:43,527
A language model drafts the thank you, the
review request, the note about the trail

194
00:11:43,567 --> 00:11:44,567
in the fall.

195
00:11:44,747 --> 00:11:47,737
An automation tool schedules
them off the checkout date.

196
00:11:48,887 --> 00:11:52,917
You do the half that decides
whether it's hospitality.

197
00:11:52,957 --> 00:11:57,177
You set the voice. You decide who
gets a message and who doesn't,

198
00:11:57,627 --> 00:12:02,137
because a guest who signed the book
expecting a thank you did not sign up for

199
00:12:02,177 --> 00:12:07,397
a drip campaign. That's what makes
six properties a one person job.

200
00:12:07,437 --> 00:12:08,887
The drafting got cheap.

201
00:12:08,927 --> 00:12:10,037
The judgment didn't.

202
00:12:10,567 --> 00:12:11,877
What do you sell first?

203
00:12:12,987 --> 00:12:15,417
Not the retainer. The audit.

204
00:12:16,427 --> 00:12:20,747
One property on a fixed fee, which in the
model is twelve hundred dollars and about

205
00:12:20,787 --> 00:12:24,287
twelve hours. You pull the site's reports

206
00:12:24,707 --> 00:12:26,037
and the property system's.

207
00:12:26,807 --> 00:12:30,117
You compute the commission line
and the ceiling on real inputs.

208
00:12:31,027 --> 00:12:34,136
You test the direct path
as a guest, on your phone.

209
00:12:35,147 --> 00:12:36,147
And

210
00:12:36,427 --> 00:12:38,297
you check one thing at the front desk.

211
00:12:38,947 --> 00:12:44,247
Does the registration card ask for
the guest's own email, or does the

212
00:12:44,287 --> 00:12:46,717
inn only ever hold the relay?

213
00:12:47,167 --> 00:12:49,397
The audit's done when
the owner has a baseline,

214
00:12:49,827 --> 00:12:53,077
the ceiling in their own numbers,
and a written recommendation.

215
00:12:53,847 --> 00:12:55,177
Sometimes that's a retainer.

216
00:12:55,527 --> 00:12:57,057
Sometimes it's audit only.

217
00:12:57,597 --> 00:13:01,887
And sometimes it's this property can't
fund it, because you're on base commission

218
00:13:01,927 --> 00:13:06,117
with a low share through the sites and
the recoverable is a few hundred a month.

219
00:13:06,347 --> 00:13:07,517
That's the product.

220
00:13:08,827 --> 00:13:09,827
It's judgment,

221
00:13:10,167 --> 00:13:12,617
and it's why the owner trusts
the second conversation.

222
00:13:13,667 --> 00:13:19,347
When the audit says yes, the retainer
is the three jobs, monthly, at a

223
00:13:19,387 --> 00:13:21,657
price under that property's ceiling.

224
00:13:22,247 --> 00:13:26,157
Findable and bookable are the
audit fixes, kept current.

225
00:13:26,807 --> 00:13:30,037
Remembered is the part
that's actually new.

226
00:13:30,467 --> 00:13:32,637
The guest book comes
back to the front desk.

227
00:13:33,007 --> 00:13:38,497
The registration card and the wifi
login ask for the guest's own email.

228
00:13:39,167 --> 00:13:44,207
Then, after checkout, a thank you and a
seasonal reason to return that fits the

229
00:13:44,247 --> 00:13:45,277
